    "finish_elapsed_s": 10.482390264980495,
    "text": "A refrigerator works by moving heat from inside to outside, using a special fluid called refrigerant. A compressor pumps this fluid through pipes, squeezing it into a hot, high-pressure gas. That gas flows through coils on the back or bottom, where it releases heat into your kitchen and turns into a liquid. Next, the liquid passes through a tiny valve into a low-pressure area, where it expands and becomes super cold. Those cold pipes run inside the fridge, absorbing heat from your food and air. Finally, the refrigerant returns to the compressor to repeat the cycle. So a fridge doesn't \"make cold\"—it carries heat away, keeping your snacks chilly!"
  }
